{"availability":"available","full":"transnetlab/transit-routing","name":"transit-routing","description":"Repository of algorithms and data for public transit routing ","category":"Other","language":"Python","stars":92,"forks":142,"created":"2021-11-04T11:30:16Z","updated":"2024-05-04T05:38:30Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":"https://transnetlab.github.io/transit-routing/html/index.html","topics":["journey-planner","kahypar","raptor","routing-algorithm"],"demo":"https://transnetlab.github.io/transit-routing/html/index.html","color":"#302e55","ink":"#dbd2ff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-09T05:49:27.477Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-09T05:49:27.477Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-09T05:49:27.477Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-09T05:49:27.477Z","stars":92,"forks":142}],"technologies":["Python"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Conventional approaches model the transit network as a time-expanded or time-dependent graph and run a variant of the Dijkstra’s algorithm. However, this method turns out to be too slow for large networks. Furthermore, while planning a journey using public transit, the number of transfers is equally important besides travel time. Popular techniques developed for PTR in the past decade include—Transfer Patterns, Connection Scan Algorithm (CSA), Round based Public Transit Routing (RAPTOR), and Trip-Based Public Transit Routing (TBTR). This repository provides various efficient algorithms to…","Our main focus is on two bicriteria approaches, RAPTOR and TBTR, with arrival time and number of transfers as the two optimization criteria. Apart from the Python implementation of already published RAPTOR, rRAPTOR, TBTR, rTBTR, and HypRAPTOR, we also include HypTBTR and their multilevel variants, such as MhypTBTR and MhypRAPTOR. Additionally, to make the two approaches more practical for solving real-world problems, the repository provides a One-To-Many version of rTBTR and rRAPTOR. These not only reduce the preprocessing times of the partitioning variants but also significantly outperform…","Switzerland's public transit network has been provided as a test case. The figure below shows the transit stop location (left) and 4-way partitioning using KaHyPar (right)."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/transnetlab/transit-routing/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"2112b16b6023f15b2608020a26575bdcd6eae401c7a77f19d3ed12d2c15b8b5c","extractedAt":"2026-10-09T05:49:27.653Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-09T05:49:27.653Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"Conventional approaches model the transit network as a time-expanded or time-dependent graph and run a variant of the Dijkstra’s algorithm. However, this method turns out to be too slow for large networks. Furthermore, while planning a journey using public transit, the number of transfers is equally important besides travel time. Popular techniques developed for PTR in the past decade include—Transfer Patterns, Connection Scan Algorithm (CSA), Round based Public Transit Routing (RAPTOR), and Trip-Based Public Transit Routing (TBTR). This repository provides various efficient algorithms to…","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/transnetlab/transit-routing/blob/main/README.md"},"audience":[],"requirements":[],"fingerprint":"2112b16b6023f15b2608020a26575bdcd6eae401c7a77f19d3ed12d2c15b8b5c","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/transnetlab/transit-routing/blob/main/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-09T05:49:27.654Z"},"overviewRepoUpdated":"2024-05-04T05:38:30Z","readmeSnapshot":{"version":1,"status":"saved","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/transnetlab/transit-routing/blob/main/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-09T05:49:27.654Z","hash":"2112b16b6023f15b2608020a26575bdcd6eae401c7a77f19d3ed12d2c15b8b5c","originalBytes":7162,"storedBytes":7162,"truncated":false,"searchTerms":["link","transit","complete","tbtr","raptor","algorithms","public","routing","transfer","csa","patterns","transportation","approaches","optimization","partitioning","rtbtr","scal